9.FILESYSTEM
/ - root directory
Essential system:
/bin - contains command-lines proograms available to all userslike ls
/sbin - contains administration command for root
/etc - core system configuration, contains configuration files of system and installed applications(no-exec)
/lib - contains necessery shared library file like /bin and /sbin work correctly
/boot - stores all files requierd to boot process including Linux Kernel and boot loaders files
User and Application Data:
/home- personal directories for euach user. where you have Desktop,Documents, appseting etc.
/root - home directory for root, separeted from /home
/opt - reserved for optional or third-party application software packages
/usr - contains userinstalled software and utilities. not hold individual users files,  have something like /usr/bin or usr/local
Dynamic and Temporary Data:
/var - stands for variable and stores files that expected to change in size and content  like /var/log caches and spool files
/tmp -storing temporary files, will be deleted after reboot
/run - Contains information about the running system since the last boot such as process ID
Device and Mount Points:
/dev - Contains special devices files that represents hardware components like input devices , Pendrive
/media - A standard mount point for removable media like USB drives
/mnt - mount point for temporarily mounting filesystem
System Information :
/proc - vitrual filestystem provides real-time information about currently running processes and kernel parameters
/srv - for site-specific data served by system, such as files or web server
VFS- Virtual File system something what can help comunication between aplications and data comunication on diffrent  filesystems (ext4 and XFS etc.),allows the Linux kernel to support numerous filesystems simultaneously without forcing users to handle different hardware interfaces directly. 
Journaling - "journaling" file is the log file on which state are the  process even after reeboot when partition check end continue work when end
Filesystem Types:
ext4 - extended filesystem up to 1 exabyte, defoult for many distribution, backword compatible with older  types
Btrfs - modern filesystem with advanced ferateres like built in snapshots, improvment performance, incremental backup
XFS - high-performance journaling to handle with large files. for large amount of data
NTFS/ FAT - standart for windows for bootable both systems
HFS+ - primary filesystem for macOS, readable , write support with additional tools
MBR Partitions - Master Boot record,traditional partition table:
- Support primary, extended and logic partitions
- Limit of four primary partitions
- to create more partitions need to be one primary must be extended partition, in this you can create manny logic partitions
- up to 2 terabytes
GPT Partitions - GIOD Partition Table, modern standard:
- only one type of partiton , can be many of them
- all partitions are assigned Globalky Unique identifier (GUID)
Filesystem Structure : 
- Boot block - first few sectors, not used by filesytem itself, contains information used to boot OS
- Superblock - after bootblock, contains data about filesystem, such size of blocks nad total size of filesystem
- Inode table - databes that manages files and directories
- Datablocks - actual content  of flies and directories stored
fdisk - partitioning tool , not support GPT
parted - tool supports both
gparted  - graphical parted
gdisk - support only GPT
/etc/fstab - configuration file, defines all available disk partitions, filesystems anddata source
df - command to check"disk free"
du - command to check disk usage
fsck - filesystem check command
inodes -  contains every information about files

10. BOOT SYSTEM	
Stage 1 BIOS
Stage 2 Bootloader
responsibility for load linux kernel, common bootloader is GRUB
Stage 3 Kernel
starts be decompressing and initializing core hardware and memory management, kernel mount root filesystem which contain all the system files.
Stage 4 Init
first process started by kernel and ancetor of all processes of system, main job is to bring system to usable state by starting services and bg processes 

11.KERNEL
Kernel primary job is to act as a bridge communicating with the hardware to execute the tasks requested by our processes.
It handle processes , memmory managment , device communication, system calls and setting up filesystem 

Kernel have Rings like levels of privileged:
Ring 0 like the most previliged access,give the full trust to manage hardware
Ring 3 level where user-mode applications run. it is the lleast privileged ring without access to hardaware

strace ls - command to look how kernel working with hardware

uname -r - kernel version
/boot - kernel storage

vmlinuz : comppressed executable Linux Kernel
initrd : temporary root files loaded into memory during startup
System.map : maps kernel function names to their memory adress
config : stores configuration settings 

lsmod - listing loaded kernel modules
sudo modprobe [NAME] - loading kernel module
sudo modprobe -r [name] - unloading kernel module

12.INIT
Primary job of Init is start and stop essential processes. There are 3 major system implementation: System V(SysV),Upstart, Systmd.
System V - proc-1 can't start without started proc-0 but with shell script it's possible
lvls of system V:
0: Shutdown
1: Single User Mode
2: Multiuser mode without networking
3: Multiuser mode with networking
4: Unused
5: Multiuser mode with networking and GUI
6: Reboot
/etc/init - init processes catalog
Upstart is init implementation on Ubuntu
Upstart works way is :
1. First loading job configurations from /etc/init.
2. Once a startup event occurs, it will run jobs triggered by that event.
3. These jobs will make new events, and then those events will trigger more jobs.
4. Upstart continues to do this until it completes all the necessary jobs.
sudo service start bluetooth - you can start, stop or restart service
sudo initctl start networkin - you can start, stop or restart jobs what are in /etc/init
systemd - default init system and service manager, responsible for initializing the system in the correct order after the kernel is loaded can be check in /usr/lib/systemd
working in system like what must be done like goals or target

